#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186216999 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217235 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "61c3724b-7fda-5547-b0cb-7bf9136fc4a3" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Gravitational Force Between Two Objects"}] :question/id #uuid "f37e7ffd-df2d-51a4-9eee-e53589011f6b" :question/math-academy-id "e-21744" :question/problem "The Sun has a mass of $(1.99\\times10^{30})\\,\\textrm{kg}$ and Mars has a mass of $(6.42\\times10^{23})\\,\\textrm{kg}.$\nThe average distance between the centers of the Sun and Mars is $(2.28\\times10^{11})\\,\\textrm{m}.$ According to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the magnitude of the gravitational force between the Sun and Mars? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **three significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G = (6.67\\times10^{-11})\\,\\textrm{m}^3/(\\textrm{kg}\\cdot\\textrm{s}^2).$" :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF = G\\dfrac{m_1m_2}{r^2}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\textrm{N}),$\n\n- $G = (6.67\\times10^{-11})\\,\\textrm{m}^3/(\\textrm{kg}\\cdot\\textrm{s}^2)$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- $m_1$ and $m_2$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\textrm{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\textrm{m}.$\n\nLet $m_1$ be the mass of the Sun and $m_2$ be the mass of Mars. Then, substituting in the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot \\frac{(1.99\\times {10}^{30})\\cdot (6.42\\times {10}^{23})}{(2.28\\times {10}^{11})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6.67\\cdot 1.99\\cdot 6.42}{(2.28)^{2}}\\times \\frac{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{30}\\cdot {10}^{23}}{({10}^{11})^{2}} \\\\  & =(16.392\\,5…)\\times {10}^{20} \\\\  & =(1.639\\,25…)\\times {10}^{21}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to three significant figures, the gravitational force between the Sun and Mars is\n$\\Big(1.64 \\times 10^{21}\\Big) \\,\\textrm{N}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217000 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217236 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd5da040-4fa3-5aeb-a98f-2a2ba03f5e16" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Mass of an Object"}] :question/id #uuid "2ef6ba7e-4f1d-51d2-af64-e1a77a7d748f" :question/math-academy-id "e-21745" :question/problem "The centers of two objects are separated by a distance of $(3.0\\times10^3)\\,\\textrm{m}$ and the magnitude of the gravitational force between them is $(3.0\\times10^{-4})\\,\\textrm{N}.$ If one object has mass $(2.0\\times10^5)\\,\\textrm{kg},$ then according to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the mass of the other object? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **two significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G = (6.67\\times10^{-11})\\,\\textrm{m}^3/(\\textrm{kg}\\cdot\\textrm{s}^2).$" :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF = G\\dfrac{m_1m_2}{r^2}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\textrm{N}),$\n\n- $G = (6.67\\times10^{-11})\\,\\textrm{m}^3/(\\textrm{kg}\\cdot\\textrm{s}^2)$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- $m_1$ and $m_2$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\textrm{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\textrm{m}.$\n\nLet $m_2$ be the unknown mass.\nFirst, let's rearrange the formula to make $m_2$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ F{r}^{2} & =G{m}_{1}{m}_{2} \\\\ {m}_{2} & =\\frac{F{r}^{2}}{G{m}_{1}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting in the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{2} & =\\frac{(3.0\\times {10}^{-4})\\cdot (3.0\\times {10}^{3})^{2}}{(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot (2.0\\times {10}^{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{3.0\\cdot (3.0)^{2}}{6.67\\cdot 2.0}\\times \\frac{{10}^{-4}\\cdot ({10}^{3})^{2}}{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{5}} \\\\  & =(2.023…)\\times {10}^{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to two significant figures, the mass of the other object is\n$\\Big(2.0 \\times 10^{8}\\Big) \\,\\textrm{kg}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217001 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217237 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "11810d4b-610c-5dac-8fe7-abf9391c2aa7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between Two Objects"}] :question/id #uuid "e7043d87-7f25-55a3-bd36-49d4ba1ae3fb" :question/math-academy-id "e-21746" :question/problem "Earth has a mass of $(5.97\\times10^{24})\\,\\textrm{kg}$ and the Moon has a mass of $(7.35\\times10^{22})\\,\\textrm{kg}.$ The magnitude of the gravitational force between them is $(1.98\\times10^{20})\\,\\textrm{N}.$ According to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the distance between the centers of mass of Earth and the Moon? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **three significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G = (6.67\\times10^{-11})\\,\\textrm{m}^3/(\\textrm{kg}\\cdot\\textrm{s}^2).$" :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF = G\\dfrac{m_1m_2}{r^2}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\textrm{N}),$\n\n- $G = (6.67\\times10^{-11})\\,\\textrm{m}^3/(\\textrm{kg}\\cdot\\textrm{s}^2)$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- $m_1$ and $m_2$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\textrm{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\textrm{m}.$\n\nFirst, let's rearrange the formula to make $r$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{F} \\\\ r & =\\sqrt{G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{F}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $m_1$ be the mass of Earth and $m_2$ be the mass of the Moon. Then, substituting in the known values, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{2} & =(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot \\frac{(5.97\\times {10}^{24})\\cdot (7.35\\times {10}^{22})}{1.98\\times {10}^{20}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6.67\\cdot 5.97\\cdot 7.35}{1.98}\\times \\frac{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{24}\\cdot {10}^{22}}{{10}^{20}} \\\\  & =(147.816…)\\times {10}^{15} \\\\  & =(14.7816…)\\times {10}^{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking the square root, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{14.7816…\\times {10}^{16}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{14.7816…}\\times \\sqrt{{10}^{16}} \\\\  & =(3.8446…)\\times {10}^{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to three significant figures, the distance between Earth and the Moon is\n$\\Big(3.84 \\times 10^{8}\\Big) \\,\\textrm{m}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217786 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217235 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "61c3724b-7fda-5547-b0cb-7bf9136fc4a3" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Gravitational Force Between Two Objects"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c4542d1-f841-5f94-9d5d-75d1cc1e760b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495684}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4c4542d1-f841-5f94-9d5d-75d1cc1e760b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495684} :answer-field/id #uuid "a25b56a7-7a1c-508b-82a2-39c31264ab50" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216585} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2273967a-61db-54d4-b614-08715aac5263" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10^{-4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495585}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2273967a-61db-54d4-b614-08715aac5263" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10^{-4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495585} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a806970-7574-5f6d-a0e0-0f388ffb134c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216586}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4f08a74f-5dc1-59fb-934d-192510b18902" :question/math-academy-id "q-334944" :question/problem "The center of two objects of masses $(7.5\\times {10}^{5})\\,\\text{kg}$ and $(2.0\\times {10}^{6})\\,\\text{kg}$ are separated by a distance of $(4.0\\times {10}^{2})\\,\\text{m}.$ According to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the magnitude of the gravitational force between the two objects? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **two significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}).$\n\nThe magnitude of the force between the two objects is\n$(${{field-1}}\n$\\times $\n{{field-2}}$)\\,\\text{N}.$" :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF=G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}),$\n\n- $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2})$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\text{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\text{m}.$\n\nChoosing ${m}_{1}=(7.5\\times {10}^{5})\\,\\text{kg}$ and ${m}_{2}=(2.0\\times {10}^{6})\\,\\text{kg}$ and substituting in the other known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot \\frac{(7.5\\times {10}^{5})\\cdot (2.0\\times {10}^{6})}{(4.0\\times {10}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6.67\\cdot 7.5\\cdot 2.0}{(4.0)^{2}}\\times \\frac{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{5}\\cdot {10}^{6}}{({10}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =6.253\\,125\\times {10}^{-4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to two significant figures, the magnitude of the gravitational force between the two objects is\n$(\\,6.3\\times {10}^{-4}\\,)\\,\\text{N}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217789 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217236 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd5da040-4fa3-5aeb-a98f-2a2ba03f5e16" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Mass of an Object"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ec869654-a121-5267-ab42-13a26a76c1f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496029}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec869654-a121-5267-ab42-13a26a76c1f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496029} :answer-field/id #uuid "8411272a-b29f-5b62-85b4-24db9b8399d2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216591} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e2cd726-85bf-5969-ac3d-c97b00b995c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10^{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495617}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e2cd726-85bf-5969-ac3d-c97b00b995c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10^{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495617} :answer-field/id #uuid "e908cf01-0b05-5721-8a0d-853b457b6c59" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216592}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "40755d2c-612a-5ccf-a761-10556a446e0e" :question/math-academy-id "q-335126" :question/problem "The average distance between the centers of Earth and the Moon is $(3.84\\times {10}^{8})\\,\\text{m}$ and the magnitude of the gravitational force between them is $(1.98\\times {10}^{20})\\,\\text{N}.$ Given that the mass of Earth is $(5.97\\times {10}^{24})\\,\\text{kg},$ then according to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the mass of the Moon? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **three significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}).$\n\nThe mass of the Moon is\n$(${{field-1}}\n$\\times $\n{{field-2}}$)\\,\\text{kg}.$" :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF=G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}),$\n\n- $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2})$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\text{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\text{m}.$\n\nLet ${m}_{1}$ be the mass of Earth and ${m}_{2}$ be the mass of the Moon.\nFirst, let's rearrange the formula to make ${m}_{2}$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ F{r}^{2} & =G{m}_{1}{m}_{2} \\\\ {m}_{2} & =\\frac{F{r}^{2}}{G{m}_{1}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting in the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{2} & =\\frac{(1.98\\times {10}^{20})\\cdot (3.84\\times {10}^{8})^{2}}{(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot (5.97\\times {10}^{24})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1.98\\cdot (3.84)^{2}}{6.67\\cdot 5.97}\\times \\frac{{10}^{20}\\cdot ({10}^{8})^{2}}{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{24}} \\\\  & =(0.733\\,2…)\\times {10}^{23} \\\\  & =(7.332…)\\times {10}^{22}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to three significant figures, the mass of the Moon is\n$(\\,7.33\\times {10}^{22}\\,)\\,\\text{kg}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217790 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217237 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "11810d4b-610c-5dac-8fe7-abf9391c2aa7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between Two Objects"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e27a8d15-7cff-52da-baac-b85166554b73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496012}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e27a8d15-7cff-52da-baac-b85166554b73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496012} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe11665f-a742-5b8a-ac1c-8d9534f7c414" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216593} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9409a4f0-af11-542e-bc53-4edd6a5bb606" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495837}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9409a4f0-af11-542e-bc53-4edd6a5bb606" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495837} :answer-field/id #uuid "011c8996-2157-52f6-9093-6078e9a4fedc" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216594}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "141575dc-d270-56a4-9e5c-9b5ce1733ede" :question/math-academy-id "q-335148" :question/problem "The gravitational force between two objects, of masses $(6.2\\times {10}^{6})\\,\\text{kg}$ and $(9.5\\times {10}^{5})\\,\\text{kg},$ has a magnitude of $(1.9\\times {10}^{-3})\\,\\text{N}.$\nAccording to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the distance between the centers of mass of the two objects? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **two significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}).$\n\nThe distance between the two objects is\n$(${{field-1}}\n$\\times $\n{{field-2}}$)\\,\\text{m}.$" :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF=G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}),$\n\n- $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2})$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\text{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\text{m}.$\n\nFirst, let's rearrange the formula to make $r$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{F} \\\\ r & =\\sqrt{G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{F}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nChoosing ${m}_{1}=(6.2\\times {10}^{6})\\,\\text{kg}$ and ${m}_{2}=(9.5\\times {10}^{5})\\,\\text{kg}$ and substituting in the other known values, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{2} & =(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot \\frac{(6.2\\times {10}^{6})\\cdot (9.5\\times {10}^{5})}{1.9\\times {10}^{-3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6.67\\cdot 6.2\\cdot 9.5}{1.9}\\times \\frac{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{6}\\cdot {10}^{5}}{{10}^{-3}} \\\\  & =206.77\\times {10}^{3} \\\\  & =20.677\\times {10}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking the square root, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{20.677\\times {10}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{20.677}\\times \\sqrt{{10}^{4}} \\\\  & =(4.547…)\\times {10}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to two significant figures, the distance between the two objects is\n$(\\,4.5\\times {10}^{2}\\,)\\,\\text{m}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496251 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217235 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "61c3724b-7fda-5547-b0cb-7bf9136fc4a3" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Gravitational Force Between Two Objects"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1363f813-3e50-54e2-b909-e2d21c495b1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495553}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1363f813-3e50-54e2-b909-e2d21c495b1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495553} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a094d86-566e-5616-90d3-312561c336e3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496077} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7fc649fe-428e-5049-a24d-21718dca4a60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10^{-4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495785}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7fc649fe-428e-5049-a24d-21718dca4a60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10^{-4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495785} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8b24067-f902-54cb-bce7-31c80a008ace" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496204}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "493d75d3-43a5-5a86-a486-dc06c6727b87" :question/math-academy-id "q-336274" :question/problem "The centers of two objects of masses $(8.0\\times {10}^{4})\\,\\text{kg}$ and $(2.5\\times {10}^{7})\\,\\text{kg}$ are separated by a distance of $(5.0\\times {10}^{2})\\,\\text{m}.$ According to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the magnitude of the gravitational force between the two objects? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **two significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}).$\n\nThe magnitude of the force between the two objects is\n$(${{field-1}}\n$\\times $\n{{field-2}}$)\\,\\text{N}.$" :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF=G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}),$\n\n- $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2})$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\text{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\text{m}.$\n\nChoosing ${m}_{1}=(8.0\\times {10}^{4})\\,\\text{kg}$ and ${m}_{2}=(2.5\\times {10}^{7})\\,\\text{kg}$ and substituting in the other known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot \\frac{(8.0\\times {10}^{4})\\cdot (2.5\\times {10}^{7})}{(5.0\\times {10}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6.67\\cdot 8.0\\cdot 2.5}{(5.0)^{2}}\\times \\frac{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{4}\\cdot {10}^{7}}{({10}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =5.336\\times {10}^{-4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to two significant figures, the magnitude of the gravitational force between the two objects is\n$(\\,5.3\\times {10}^{-4}\\,)\\,\\text{N}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311673 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217235 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "61c3724b-7fda-5547-b0cb-7bf9136fc4a3" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Gravitational Force Between Two Objects"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ad602a92-a4e1-55b3-a5f2-ad4a71149f37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310149}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ad602a92-a4e1-55b3-a5f2-ad4a71149f37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310149} :answer-field/id #uuid "50cb604b-e0fb-5afa-b0cc-f5ebfbeca6b3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311002} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36ff45d5-e2a8-553c-8663-3825a63e87a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{10}^{-4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309166} {:answer/id #uuid "d6cad4c9-c729-5998-84f4-108ee9f841da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10^{-4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495982}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6cad4c9-c729-5998-84f4-108ee9f841da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10^{-4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495982} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0ad518d-938b-547f-bb69-a628280fe3fb" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311446}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fdaf854a-23e0-590a-afed-80c02813eae0" :question/math-academy-id "q-336275" :question/problem "The centers of two objects of masses $(6.5\\times {10}^{5})\\,\\text{kg}$ and $(4.2\\times {10}^{6})\\,\\text{kg}$ are separated by a distance of $(7.5\\times {10}^{2})\\,\\text{m}.$ According to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the magnitude of the gravitational force between the two objects? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **two significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}).$\n\nThe magnitude of the force between the two objects is\n$(${{field-1}}\n$\\times $\n{{field-2}}$)\\,\\text{N}.$" :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF=G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}),$\n\n- $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2})$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\text{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\text{m}.$\n\nChoosing ${m}_{1}=(6.5\\times {10}^{5})\\,\\text{kg}$ and ${m}_{2}=(4.2\\times {10}^{6})\\,\\text{kg}$ and substituting in the other known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot \\frac{(6.5\\times {10}^{5})\\cdot (4.2\\times {10}^{6})}{(7.5\\times {10}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6.67\\cdot 6.5\\cdot 4.2}{(7.5)^{2}}\\times \\frac{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{5}\\cdot {10}^{6}}{({10}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =3.237\\,173…\\times {10}^{-4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to two significant figures, the magnitude of the gravitational force between the two objects is\n$(\\,3.2\\times {10}^{-4}\\,)\\,\\text{N}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496326 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217235 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "61c3724b-7fda-5547-b0cb-7bf9136fc4a3" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Gravitational Force Between Two Objects"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ea92853-8daa-5326-97ac-7b18c50fa0c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10^{23}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495546}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ea92853-8daa-5326-97ac-7b18c50fa0c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10^{23}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495546} :answer-field/id #uuid "056edfa7-0fe2-5df2-b784-93349b9866c4" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496074} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b56e2ef-3ed6-5e05-80da-4f4b6f83b676" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495539}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b56e2ef-3ed6-5e05-80da-4f4b6f83b676" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495539} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2e108d7-70bb-59b2-b1bd-5f0398863020" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496172}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e90c4b66-398a-540c-92b6-fce26b283a8e" :question/math-academy-id "q-336279" :question/problem "The Sun has a mass of $(1.99\\times {10}^{30})\\,\\text{kg}$ and Jupiter has a mass of $(1.90\\times {10}^{27})\\,\\text{kg}.$\nThe average distance between the centers of the Sun and Jupiter is $(7.78\\times {10}^{11})\\,\\text{m}.$ According to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the magnitude of the gravitational force between the Sun and Jupiter? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **three significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}).$\n\nThe magnitude of the force between the Sun and Jupiter is\n$(${{field-1}}\n$\\times $\n{{field-2}}$)\\,\\text{N}.$" :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF=G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}),$\n\n- $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2})$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\text{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\text{m}.$\n\nLet ${m}_{1}$ be the mass of the Sun and ${m}_{2}$ be the mass of Jupiter. Then, substituting in the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot \\frac{(1.99\\times {10}^{30})\\cdot (1.90\\times {10}^{27})}{(7.78\\times {10}^{11})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6.67\\cdot 1.99\\cdot 1.90}{(7.78)^{2}}\\times \\frac{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{30}\\cdot {10}^{27}}{({10}^{11})^{2}} \\\\  & =(0.416\\,651…)\\times {10}^{24} \\\\  & =(4.166\\,51…)\\times {10}^{23}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to three significant figures, the gravitational force between the Sun and Jupiter is\n$(\\,4.17\\times {10}^{23}\\,)\\,\\text{N}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496317 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217236 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd5da040-4fa3-5aeb-a98f-2a2ba03f5e16" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Mass of an Object"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "721a9499-1bdd-54f3-ba07-68ff1b7cc73d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10^{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495760}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "721a9499-1bdd-54f3-ba07-68ff1b7cc73d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10^{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495760} :answer-field/id #uuid "30b84e28-42b9-5c46-b2ac-bd1eafb073ab" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496096} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e0c0b35b-00e3-5caa-b1bf-990bd59ede3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496008}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e0c0b35b-00e3-5caa-b1bf-990bd59ede3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496008} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d4ed601-5f68-5199-815d-6a11c05969c8" :