n, substituting in the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{2} & =\\frac{(4.0\\times {10}^{-6})\\cdot (3.0\\times {10}^{3})^{2}}{(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot (2.5\\times {10}^{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{4.0\\cdot (3.0)^{2}}{6.67\\cdot 2.5}\\times \\frac{{10}^{-6}\\cdot ({10}^{3})^{2}}{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{5}} \\\\  & =(2.158…)\\times {10}^{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to two significant figures, the mass of the other object is\n$(\\,2.2\\times {10}^{6}\\,)\\,\\text{kg}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496229 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217236 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd5da040-4fa3-5aeb-a98f-2a2ba03f5e16" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Mass of an Object"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d555e08f-1ba8-52b9-aa15-5b15cb8c3133" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.97" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495977}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d555e08f-1ba8-52b9-aa15-5b15cb8c3133" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.97" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495977} :answer-field/id #uuid "04814265-845a-5b0c-b0e7-4e78d8ce847c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496073} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9df31d83-b9cd-52dd-aa9a-df445bb72d51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10^{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495864}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9df31d83-b9cd-52dd-aa9a-df445bb72d51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10^{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495864} :answer-field/id #uuid "55511cd4-782c-5aba-a839-f537625df779" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496116}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "10fc12bd-51f2-5325-ac89-b383736a7b6d" :question/math-academy-id "q-336292" :question/problem "The average distance between the centers of the Sun and Earth is $(1.50\\times {10}^{11})\\,\\text{m}$ and the magnitude of the gravitational force between them is $(3.52\\times {10}^{22})\\,\\text{N}.$ Given that the mass of the Sun is $(1.99\\times {10}^{30})\\,\\text{kg},$ then according to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the mass of Earth? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **three significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}).$\n\nThe mass of Earth is\n$(${{field-1}}\n$\\times $\n{{field-2}}$)\\,\\text{kg}.$" :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF=G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}),$\n\n- $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2})$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\text{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\text{m}.$\n\nLet ${m}_{1}$ be the mass of the Sun and ${m}_{2}$ be the mass of Earth.\nFirst, let's rearrange the formula to make ${m}_{2}$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ F{r}^{2} & =G{m}_{1}{m}_{2} \\\\ {m}_{2} & =\\frac{F{r}^{2}}{G{m}_{1}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting in the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{2} & =\\frac{(3.52\\times {10}^{22})\\cdot (1.50\\times {10}^{11})^{2}}{(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot (1.99\\times {10}^{30})} \\\\  & =\\frac{3.52\\cdot (1.50)^{2}}{6.67\\cdot 1.99}\\times \\frac{{10}^{22}\\cdot ({10}^{11})^{2}}{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{30}} \\\\  & =(0.596\\,68…)\\times {10}^{25} \\\\  & =(5.9668…)\\times {10}^{24}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to three significant figures, the mass of Earth is\n$(\\,5.97\\times {10}^{24}\\,)\\,\\text{kg}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311680 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217236 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd5da040-4fa3-5aeb-a98f-2a2ba03f5e16" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Mass of an Object"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "085a39b8-34b4-59be-bf78-3ad89c9cee72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{10}^{23}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308811} {:answer/id #uuid "81d5236d-2a63-5ceb-afd9-2c4d4fc7fd6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10^{23}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495787}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "81d5236d-2a63-5ceb-afd9-2c4d4fc7fd6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10^{23}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495787} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f64e729-bdd6-5060-8792-c766db195183" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311089} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01b78827-bc2a-5019-be53-c37837890ae7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308759}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01b78827-bc2a-5019-be53-c37837890ae7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308759} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb7af761-a937-5f8b-85e7-5f8e2b17cdfd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311330}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a6fc2d2a-8149-567b-ab06-063d27b8e5bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-336295" :question/problem "The average distance between the centers of the Sun and Mars is $(2.28\\times {10}^{11})\\,\\text{m}$ and the magnitude of the gravitational force between them is $(1.64\\times {10}^{21})\\,\\text{N}.$ Given that the mass of the Sun is $(1.99\\times {10}^{30})\\,\\text{kg},$ then according to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the mass of Mars? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **three significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}).$\n\nThe mass of Mars is\n$(${{field-1}}\n$\\times $\n{{field-2}}$)\\,\\text{kg}.$" :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF=G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}),$\n\n- $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2})$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\text{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\text{m}.$\n\nLet ${m}_{1}$ be the mass of the Sun and ${m}_{2}$ be the mass of Mars.\nFirst, let's rearrange the formula to make ${m}_{2}$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ F{r}^{2} & =G{m}_{1}{m}_{2} \\\\ {m}_{2} & =\\frac{F{r}^{2}}{G{m}_{1}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting in the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{2} & =\\frac{(1.64\\times {10}^{21})\\cdot (2.28\\times {10}^{11})^{2}}{(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot (1.99\\times {10}^{30})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1.64\\cdot (2.28)^{2}}{6.67\\cdot 1.99}\\times \\frac{{10}^{21}\\cdot ({10}^{11})^{2}}{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{30}} \\\\  & =(0.642\\,29…)\\times {10}^{24} \\\\  & =(6.4229…)\\times {10}^{23}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to three significant figures, the mass of Mars is\n$(\\,6.42\\times {10}^{23}\\,)\\,\\text{kg}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496263 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217237 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "11810d4b-610c-5dac-8fe7-abf9391c2aa7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between Two Objects"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b94d79a1-ce8f-5c68-a1b6-dc593f77b4aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495923}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b94d79a1-ce8f-5c68-a1b6-dc593f77b4aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495923} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab029381-1106-558f-825e-416a0dcfa862" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496157} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88b8cfab-89fd-5247-b8a8-50c69594c75b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495807}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "88b8cfab-89fd-5247-b8a8-50c69594c75b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495807} :answer-field/id #uuid "f859ec4a-9e84-5b08-b237-cdd046aaba2f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496214}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5f6f1f55-e6a2-5e50-9cd8-7d4ea9cc100b" :question/math-academy-id "q-336301" :question/problem "The gravitational force between two objects, of masses $(8.2\\times {10}^{5})\\,\\text{kg}$ and $(5.6\\times {10}^{6})\\,\\text{kg},$ has a magnitude of $(2.0\\times {10}^{-6})\\,\\text{N}.$\nAccording to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the distance between the centers of mass of the two objects? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **two significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}).$\n\nThe distance between the two objects is\n$(${{field-1}}\n$\\times $\n{{field-2}}$)\\,\\text{m}.$" :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF=G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}),$\n\n- $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2})$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\text{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\text{m}.$\n\nFirst, let's rearrange the formula to make $r$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{F} \\\\ r & =\\sqrt{G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{F}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nChoosing ${m}_{1}=(8.2\\times {10}^{5})\\,\\text{kg}$ and ${m}_{2}=(5.6\\times {10}^{6})\\,\\text{kg}$ and substituting in the other known values, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{2} & =(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot \\frac{(8.2\\times {10}^{5})\\cdot (5.6\\times {10}^{6})}{2.0\\times {10}^{-6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6.67\\cdot 8.2\\cdot 5.6}{2.0}\\times \\frac{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{5}\\cdot {10}^{6}}{{10}^{-6}} \\\\  & =153.1432\\times {10}^{6} \\\\  & =1.531432\\times {10}^{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking the square root, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{1.531432\\times {10}^{8}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1.531432}\\times \\sqrt{{10}^{8}} \\\\  & =(1.2375…)\\times {10}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to two significant figures, the distance between the two objects is\n$(\\,1.2\\times {10}^{4}\\,)\\,\\text{m}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311686 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217237 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "11810d4b-610c-5dac-8fe7-abf9391c2aa7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between Two Objects"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bcbb534d-2c92-5c2f-a8fe-e17d3ad2fd0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310278}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bcbb534d-2c92-5c2f-a8fe-e17d3ad2fd0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310278} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c8396d6-798a-55e6-bbe8-0d0f07b1c64a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311034} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "de2407ae-b970-55d0-82cd-097b26f2d840" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{10}^{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310529} {:answer/id #uuid "d2b93c2d-2e63-5a33-b47a-3706073910a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10^{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495971}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d2b93c2d-2e63-5a33-b47a-3706073910a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10^{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495971} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6f47661-d725-5de6-a866-704c55c0d262" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311321}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4734863f-a900-55bb-995a-471d13e8ef2d" :question/math-academy-id "q-336307" :question/problem "The Sun has a mass of $(1.99\\times {10}^{30})\\,\\text{kg}$ and Neptune has a mass of $(1.02\\times {10}^{26})\\,\\text{kg}.$ The magnitude of the gravitational force between them is $(6.69\\times {10}^{20})\\,\\text{N}.$ According to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the distance between the centers of mass of the Sun and Neptune? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **three significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}).$\n\nThe distance between the Sun and Neptune is\n$(${{field-1}}\n$\\times $\n{{field-2}}$)\\,\\text{m}.$" :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF=G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}),$\n\n- $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2})$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\text{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\text{m}.$\n\nFirst, let's rearrange the formula to make $r$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{F} \\\\ r & =\\sqrt{G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{F}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet ${m}_{1}$ be the mass of the Sun and ${m}_{2}$ be the mass of Neptune. Then, substituting in the known values, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{2} & =(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot \\frac{(1.99\\times {10}^{30})\\cdot (1.02\\times {10}^{26})}{6.69\\times {10}^{20}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6.67\\cdot 1.99\\cdot 1.02}{6.69}\\times \\frac{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{30}\\cdot {10}^{26}}{{10}^{20}} \\\\  & =(2.0237…)\\times {10}^{25} \\\\  & =(20.237…)\\times {10}^{24}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking the square root, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{20.237…\\times {10}^{24}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{20.237…}\\times \\sqrt{{10}^{24}} \\\\  & =(4.498…)\\times {10}^{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to three significant figures, the distance between the Sun and Neptune is\n$(\\,4.50\\times {10}^{12}\\,)\\,\\text{m}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496242 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217237 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "11810d4b-610c-5dac-8fe7-abf9391c2aa7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between Two Objects"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b0a01ef6-9c9b-5d09-92b6-aaa9a3b3eb9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495904}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b0a01ef6-9c9b-5d09-92b6-aaa9a3b3eb9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495904} :answer-field/id #uuid "65c170d4-db55-5f82-97f4-a86ba579f024" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496129} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f40bb757-19ed-5511-89eb-9d717cb3ac9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10^{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496041}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f40bb757-19ed-5511-89eb-9d717cb3ac9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10^{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496041} :answer-field/id #uuid "fdfc0d2a-f06a-5c20-bd50-0265e3825c1e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496220}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "39855b88-dc8a-571a-b435-84e158ee3c93" :question/math-academy-id "q-336310" :question/problem "Mars has a mass of $(6.42\\times {10}^{23})\\,\\text{kg}$ and Jupiter has a mass of $(1.90\\times {10}^{27})\\,\\text{kg}.$ The magnitude of the gravitational force between them is $(2.69\\times {10}^{17})\\,\\text{N}.$ According to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the distance between the centers of mass of Mars and Jupiter? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **three significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}).$\n\nThe distance between Mars and Jupiter is\n$(${{field-1}}\n$\\times $\n{{field-2}}$)\\,\\text{m}.$" :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF=G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}),$\n\n- $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2})$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\text{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\text{m}.$\n\nFirst, let's rearrange the formula to make $r$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{F} \\\\ r & =\\sqrt{G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{F}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet ${m}_{1}$ be the mass of Mars and ${m}_{2}$ be the mass of Jupiter. Then, substituting in the known values, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{2} & =(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot \\frac{(6.42\\times {10}^{23})\\cdot (1.90\\times {10}^{27})}{2.69\\times {10}^{17}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6.67\\cdot 6.42\\cdot 1.90}{2.69}\\times \\frac{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{23}\\cdot {10}^{27}}{{10}^{17}} \\\\  & =(30.245…)\\times {10}^{22}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking the square root, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{30.245…\\times {10}^{22}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{30.245…}\\times \\sqrt{{10}^{22}} \\\\  & =(5.499…)\\times {10}^{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to three significant figures, the distance between Mars and Jupiter is\n$(\\,5.50\\times {10}^{11}\\,)\\,\\text{m}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311688 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217237 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "11810d4b-610c-5dac-8fe7-abf9391c2aa7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between Two Objects"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b0658bcd-109d-5074-ae2f-ffd4d820d520" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{10}^{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310172} {:answer/id #uuid "e3ea943a-0fa9-595e-a445-93f5966895e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10^{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496015}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e3ea943a-0fa9-595e-a445-93f5966895e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10^{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496015} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e72599a-c501-53ec-bc0d-dc66e0a2c2cb" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310961} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97659834-b505-529e-8bad-43eb98c2047b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.08" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309961}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97659834-b505-529e-8bad-43eb98c2047b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.08" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309961} :answer-field/id #uuid "92734986-b058-5cad-a184-d151f6495698" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311209}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4ad559c9-9cfd-5c87-86a0-09ca886a809d" :question/math-academy-id "q-336314" :question/problem "The Sun has a mass of $(1.99\\times {10}^{30})\\,\\text{kg}$ and Venus has a mass of $(4.87\\times {10}^{24})\\,\\text{kg}.$ The magnitude of the gravitational force between them is $(5.54\\times {10}^{22})\\,\\text{N}.$ According to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the distance between the centers of mass of the Sun and Venus? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **three significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}).$\n\nThe distance between the Sun and Venus is\n$(${{field-1}}\n$\\times $\n{{field-2}}$)\\,\\text{m}.$" :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF=G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}),$\n\n- $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2})$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\text{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\text{m}.$\n\nFirst, let's rearrange the formula to make $r$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{F} \\\\ r & =\\sqrt{G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{F}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet ${m}_{1}$ be the mass of the Sun and ${m}_{2}$ be the mass of Venus. Then, substituting in the known values, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{2} & =(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot \\frac{(1.99\\times {10}^{30})\\cdot (4.87\\times {10}^{24})}{5.54\\times {10}^{22}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6.67\\cdot 1.99\\cdot 4.87}{5.54}\\times \\frac{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{30}\\cdot {10}^{24}}{{10}^{22}} \\\\  & =(11.668…)\\times {10}^{21} \\\\  & =(1.1668…)\\times {10}^{22}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking the square root, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{1.1668…\\times {10}^{22}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1.1668…}\\times \\sqrt{{10}^{22}} \\\\  & =(1.0801…)\\times {10}^{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to three significant figures, the distance between the Sun and Venus is\n$(\\,1.08\\times {10}^{11}\\,)\\,\\text{m}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496224 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217236 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd5da040-4fa3-5aeb-a98f-2a2ba03f5e16" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Mass of an Object"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33a7f1f8-6729-5ad0-9c4b-a47c781a9a18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.90" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495631}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33a7f1f8-6729-5ad0-9c4b-a47c781a9a18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.90" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495631} :answer-field/id #uuid "319135a4-a585-5cc5-a698-7e9d448442eb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496097} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d41cf47-1099-53e9-af73-ba7e7af55d50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10^{27}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495723}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5d41cf47-1099-53e9-af73-ba7e7af55d50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10^{27}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495723} :answer-field/id #uuid "4420d3e9-6356-5714-a483-a1b091a3e136" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496106}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "047c28c1-e17d-56d3-9e40-ea3f7e7b2867" :question/math-academy-id "q-336317" :question/problem "The average distance between the centers of Earth and Jupiter is $(6.29\\times {10}^{11})\\,\\text{m}$ and the magnitude of the gravitational force between them is $(1.91\\times {10}^{18})\\,\\text{N}.$ Given that the mass of Earth is $(5.97\\times {10}^{24})\\,\\text{kg},$ then according to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the mass of Jupiter? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **three significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}).$\n\nThe mass of Jupiter is\n$(${{field-1}}\n$\\times $\n{{field-2}}$)\\,\\text{kg}.$" :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF=G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}),$\n\n- $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2})$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\text{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\text{m}.$\n\nLet ${m}_{1}$ be the mass of Earth and ${m}_{2}$ be the mass of Jupiter.\nFirst, let's rearrange the formula to make ${m}_{2}$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ F{r}^{2} & =G{m}_{1}{m}_{2} \\\\ {m}_{2} & =\\frac{F{r}^{2}}{G{m}_{1}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting in the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{2} & =\\frac{(1.91\\times {10}^{18})\\cdot (6.29\\times {10}^{11})^{2}}{(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot (5.97\\times {10}^{24})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1.91\\cdot (6.29)^{2}}{6.67\\cdot 5.97}\\times \\frac{{10}^{18}\\cdot ({10}^{11})^{2}}{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{24}} \\\\  & =(1.8977…)\\times {10}^{27}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to three significant figures, the mass of Jupiter is\n$(\\,1.90\\times {10}^{27}\\,)\\,\\text{kg}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496241 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217235 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "61c3724b-7fda-5547-b0cb-7bf9136fc4a3" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Gravitational Force Between Two Objects"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4316b880-3390-548a-94ed-d01f94de3827" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10^{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495663}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4316b880-3390-548a-94ed-d01f94de3827" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10^{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495663} :answer-field/id #uuid "405034c0-e463-548e-91c7-9b989518ba36" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496103} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5665ada4-61bf-5bd3-92fa-4ad1b9c93238" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495708}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5665ada4-61bf-5bd3-92fa-4ad1b9c93238" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495708} :answer-field/id #uuid "93780ef7-4739-5931-a952-6ca27bf8e53f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496148}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "36c5d3a3-e17d-52d4-a3d2-5e187ff26b13" :question/math-academy-id "q-336320" :question/problem "Earth has a mass of $(5.97\\times {10}^{24})\\,\\text{kg}$ and Mars has a mass of $(6.42\\times {10}^{23})\\,\\text{kg}.$\nThe average distance between the centers of Earth and Mars is $(7.83\\times {10}^{10})\\,\\text{m}.$ According to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the magnitude of the gravitational force between Earth and Mars? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **three significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}).$\n\nThe magnitude of the force between Earth and Mars is\n$(${{field-1}}\n$\\times $\n{{field-2}}$)\\,\\text{N}.$" :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF=G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}),$\n\n- $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2})$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\text{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\text{m}.$\n\nLet ${m}_{1}$ be the mass of Earth and ${m}_{2}$ be the mass of Mars. Then, substituting in the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot \\frac{(5.97\\times {10}^{24})\\cdot (6.42\\times {10}^{23})}{(7.83\\times {10}^{10})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6.67\\cdot 5.97\\cdot 6.42}{(7.83)^{2}}\\times \\frac{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{24}\\cdot {10}^{23}}{({10}^{10})^{2}} \\\\  & =(4.169\\,76…)\\times {10}^{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to three significant figures, the gravitational force between Earth and Mars is\n$(\\,4.17\\times {10}^{16}\\,)\\,\\text{N}.$"}]}
